What’s really going on

From the issues you linked:

Python 3.13’s ssl.create_default_context() now enables VERIFY_X509_STRICT by default. 
bugzilla.redhat.com
+1

That causes OpenSSL to enforce RFC 5280 much more strictly: certificates must have proper Authority Key Identifier (AKI) / Subject Key Identifier (SKI) relationships, etc.

Many internal / corporate CAs and proxies issue certs that are “good enough” for normal verification but missing AKI or otherwise not fully RFC-compliant. Those now fail with:

SSLCertVerificationError: ... certificate verify failed: Missing Authority Key Identifier (_ssl.c:1032) 
GitHub
+1

The OpenSSL issue you linked is therefore marked “resolved: not a bug” – OpenSSL is just doing what it’s told in strict mode. 
GitHub

So to “fix” it, you either:

Fix your certificates (recommended), or

Turn off strict verification in your client (workaround / less secure).

1. Proper fix: fix your CA / proxy certificates

You need to make your internal CA + any intermediates RFC 5280-compliant:

Inspect the chain your client sees

openssl s_client -connect your-proxy:443 -showcerts </dev/null


Save each cert to a file (cert0.pem, cert1.pem, …) and inspect:

openssl x509 -in cert0.pem -noout -text | sed -n '60,120p'


Check for:

X509v3 Subject Key Identifier

X509v3 Authority Key Identifier

For every non-self-signed CA or intermediate in the chain:

It must have an Authority Key Identifier that points to the issuer (usually matches the issuer’s Subject Key Identifier, or contains the issuer DN + serial).

If it’s missing, that’s what triggers Missing Authority Key Identifier.

Update your CA / proxy config (OpenSSL example) to include:

[ v3_ca ]
subjectKeyIdentifier=hash
authorityKeyIdentifier=keyid:always,issuer
basicConstraints = critical, CA:true
keyUsage = critical, keyCertSign, cRLSign


Then re-issue the CA/intermediate certs and any leaf certs as needed, redeploy them on your proxy / internal PKI.

This is exactly how other projects fixed it when they hit the same error – they added proper SKI/AKI extensions to their generated certs. 
Launchpad
+1

Once the chain is RFC-compliant, VERIFY_X509_STRICT will pass without any client-side hacks.

2. Workaround: disable VERIFY_X509_STRICT in your Python client

If you can’t fix the certs immediately and just need things working while infra catches up, you can turn off strict verification in your client code.

a) Using urllib.request

From a Python core dev suggestion: 
Discussions on Python.org

import ssl
import urllib.request

ctx = ssl.create_default_context()
ctx.verify_flags &= ~ssl.VERIFY_X509_STRICT   # remove strict flag

with urllib.request.urlopen("https://www.google.com", context=ctx) as r:
    print(r.status, r.read()[:100])

b) Using requests (via a custom adapter)
import ssl
import requests
from urllib3.util import ssl_ as urllib3_ssl
from requests.adapters import HTTPAdapter

class NonStrictHTTPSAdapter(HTTPAdapter):
    def init_poolmanager(self, *args, **kwargs):
        ctx = urllib3_ssl.create_urllib3_context()
        ctx.verify_flags &= ~ssl.VERIFY_X509_STRICT
        kwargs["ssl_context"] = ctx
        return super().init_poolmanager(*args, **kwargs)

session = requests.Session()
session.mount("https://", NonStrictHTTPSAdapter())

r = session.get(
    "https://www.google.com",
    verify="/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt",  # your CA bundle
    # proxies=... if needed
)
print(r.status_code, r.text[:100])


This keeps normal certificate verification, but removes the strict RFC 5280 checks that are choking on your broken AKI.

⚠️ Security note: Disabling VERIFY_X509_STRICT is safer than completely disabling verification, but still weakens checks. It should be treated as a temporary workaround until your PKI is fixed.

3. Quick escape hatches (not ideal)

If you just need a “it must work right now” hack for some internal tooling:

Run it with Python 3.12 instead of 3.13 (no strict flag by default). 
GitHub
+1

Or completely disable verification (verify=False in requests, PYTHONHTTPSVERIFY=0, etc.) – but this is insecure and usually a bad idea except in a lab.

TL;DR

OpenSSL issue 28391 is closed as “not a bug” because Python 3.13’s strict mode is correctly rejecting a malformed certificate chain. 
GitHub
+1

Real fix: regenerate your internal CA / proxy certs so every CA / intermediate has proper SKI/AKI per RFC 5280.

Workaround: in Python, create a custom SSLContext and do:

ctx.verify_flags &= ~ssl.VERIFY_X509_STRICT
